{% trace-scenario title="Why was this answer wrong?" prompt="A RAG chatbot gives a confident answer, but the source documents do not support it. Which span should you inspect first?" outcome="Compare the user question, retrieved passages, ranking scores, prompt context, and final answer as one causal chain." /%}

## At a glance

This guide is for **developers building production RAG chatbots**. It helps you **separate retrieval failures from generation failures**. It does not prescribe a specific vector database or embedding provider.

### The RAG debugging loop

Use **Question → Retrieve → Ground → Answer → Evaluate**. Keep each stage observable; otherwise a wrong answer collapses into one opaque model call.

## Define what the chatbot is allowed to answer

Write the source boundary before choosing a model. State which corpus is authoritative, how fresh it must be, and what the chatbot should say when evidence is missing. The [RAG evaluation guide](/blog/rag-evaluation-guide) gives a practical way to measure retrieval and answer quality instead of treating fluency as correctness.

## Trace retrieval separately from generation

Capture the query, filters, top-k setting, document identifiers, chunk boundaries, scores, prompt version, and answer. Do not store sensitive document text by default; retain identifiers and controlled previews where possible.

| Failure symptom | Likely stage | First evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Relevant source never appears | Retrieval | Query, filters, top-k, scores |
| Source appears but answer ignores it | Grounding | Context and prompt version |
| Answer contradicts source | Generation or policy | Citation and output validation |
| Answer is stale | Ingestion | Document version and index timestamp |

## Make unsupported answers visible

Require citations or source identifiers when the task needs factual grounding. Validate that cited identifiers came from retrieved context. A refusal or “I do not have enough evidence” response is often safer than filling a gap with plausible language. Pair this with [LLM hallucination detection](/blog/llm-hallucination-detection) when you need a review queue for unsupported claims.

## Test the whole path

Build a fixture set containing answerable questions, ambiguous questions, stale documents, adversarial instructions, empty retrieval, and conflicting sources. Compare retrieval recall, groundedness, answer quality, latency, and cost together. A change that improves one number while damaging the user task is not an improvement.

## Release checklist

- Define the authoritative corpus and freshness boundary.
- Record retrieval inputs and document identifiers.
- Test empty, stale, conflicting, and adversarial context.
- Validate citations against retrieved sources.
- Inspect representative traces before changing the prompt or index.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="Does RAG eliminate hallucinations?" answer="No. Retrieval gives the model additional evidence but does not guarantee that the answer follows it. Validate grounding, citations, and refusal behavior." /%}
{% faq-item question="How many chunks should a RAG chatbot retrieve?" answer="There is no universal value. Tune top-k against a representative evaluation set and inspect latency, context quality, duplication, and answer correctness together." /%}
{% faq-item question="What is the most useful RAG trace field?" answer="The document identifier and retrieval score are a strong starting point because they let you distinguish missing evidence from an answer that ignored available evidence." /%}

